            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

AIR SEALING



Overall, your house is moderately leaky from an air leakage perspective, and I have identified a few locations that can be addressed:



** The largest source is the open combustion air pipe (5" in diameter) located beside the furnace, looking like part of the ducting - this pipe is likely a significant cause of this room being cold. With a furnace upgrade to a new condensing furnace (propane or oil) it would eliminate the combustion air pipe.



** Another significant source of air leakage is likely through the belly bag. When using the IR camera during the blower door test I observed cold air being pulled into your home via the furnace ducts. The furnace ducts in this style of home provide heat to the belly bag. If I observe them pulling in cold air when I do the blower door test this tells me there are areas in the belly bag in need of repair. I was unable to verify this visually as access to the crawlspace was quite tight. 



** Some windows are leaky around the frames, or do not latch well, and should be addressed with a full frame window replacement. See Window upgrade section for more details.



** The main bathroom exhaust fan was a bit leaky when the home was depressurized. Bathroom fans can be replaced with energy star rated fans with butterfly style dampers, that seal better than traditional exhaust fan dampers.



** Air leakage was observed around the outside edge of all exterior doors. Consider replacing weather stripping as needed, and ensure the latches are working as intended. Regularly levelling your home, can also help ensure doors are fitting properly in the door jam.



** Some electrical outlets along exterior walls were leaky. You can remove the face plates and install foam gaskets behind, to help with this small source of air leakage.



Savings: If you address the air leakage locations identified above, I estimate a modest 16% reduction in air leakage heat loss, but it could result in a larger reduction. This is estimated to save you $350 per year and decrease your annual energy usage by 11 GJ. Although the savings aren't huge, the increase in comfort downstairs will be noticeable, for relatively low effort/ expense.



There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for performing air sealing, however you will enjoy the annual energy savings every year.



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant between $715 and $1300 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on the level achieved. This is one of the most cost-effective energy-saving measures you can undertake.



An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

MAIN WALLS



The main exterior walls of your home, were estimated to be of 2x3 + 2x4 double stud construction around the entire home, with batt insulation in the cavities. Without opening up your wall this cannot be verified, but based on our discussion at your home, and my measurements of wall thickness, this seems to be a reasonable estimate. Since you mentioned that you are considering completing a siding upgrade on your home, I have modelled adding 3 additional inches (R15) of rigid extruded polystyrene insulation to every exterior surface. This R-value of insulation would bring your homes insulation value up to current building practice values. Due to the large amount of labour involved in re-siding a home, and the high cost, this recommendation should only be taken in conjunction with a siding upgrade.



Savings: By adding 3 inches (R15) of rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to all exterior walls, you could potentially save $ 450 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 14 GJ, and qualify for up to a $2,500 rebate from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program ÂinsulationÂ rebate, a pre-approval is required and my EnerGuide evaluation of your home satisfies the pre-approval requirement. The rebate application, copies of sales receipts, and photos that clearly show where the insulation was added (and the thickness) must be received within three months of completing your insulation upgrade. For a full rebate, insulation products must be purchased in the Northwest Territories. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of up to $4,940 is available for adding insulation to exterior wall area of your home. Insulation grants are based on the percentage of wall area to which the insulation has been added and the amount of insulation added. A minimum of 20% of the exterior wall area of your home, excluding foundation walls, must be insulated in order to qualify for a grant.



ARCTIC ENERGY ALLIANCE - DEEP HOME ENERGY RETROFIT PROGRAM



If you choose to insulate the exterior walls of your home with a minimum of R10 effective to a minimum of 20% of the wall area, you may qualify for AEA's Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program. By upgrading your exterior walls, you unlock the potential for more rebate dollars, for other products. PLEASE CALL ME IF YOU ARE INTERESTED IN THIS PROGRAM, to ensure funding is available for you before you begin. Funding is available on a first come, first served basis, and projects must be complete by January 2024.

</mainWalls>
                <windows>

WINDOWS



The windows in your house are older windows of similar style throughout the home. Most windows are double paned, wood framed windows, some with low e coatings, some without. A few windows show signs of blown seals between panes, meaning any Argon originally in there may have escaped.



Upgrading specific windows in this house to ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3 is something that could be considered as an upgrade for this home. If this is something the homeowner wishes to pursue I recommend full frame replacement windows to vinyl frames that are triple pane, double low-E glazed, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 1.22 W/m2K.



Savings: By replacing all 12 windows in the home with ones that meet the criteria described above, you could potentially save $490 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 15 GJ. 



There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program, however, there are rebates available from the Canada Greener Homes Grant. Please see their website for more details on program requirements.



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, grants of either $162.50 or $325.00 per window rough opening are available when an ENERGY STAR certified or ENERGY STAR certified Most Efficient window is installed to replace an existing window. Replacing old, damaged or leaky windows with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy, improve comfort and reduce noise. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.

</windows>
                <doors>

DOORS



The exterior doors in your home are older, steel doors with polystyrene cores, and are considered to be in fair condition.  Weather stripping was noted to be in need of repair at all locations, and should be inspected annually. During the blower door test I observed cold air being pulled into the home around the edge of the door. When I modelled upgrading to new highly efficient doors, the savings were minimal, so I would instead recommend focussing your efforts on improving the weather stripping, or consider levelling your home. There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT for door replacements.



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $162.50 per hinged door system is available when an eligible ENERGY STAR certified door or door system is installed. Replacing old, damaged, or leaky hinged doors with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy and improve comfort. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.

</doors>
                <HotWaterSystem>

Propane Instantaneous Upgrade from Electric



You currently have a 182 litre conventional electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly. You may want to consider replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.95 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.95, you could potentially save approximately $1040 per year in energy costs, and qualify for up to $750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

FURNACE UPGRADE



Your current oil furnace is an older, 83% efficient, furnace with an open combustion air supply (floor vent to the right of the furnace) which means during the combustion process the furnace interacts with interior air. After our discussion, it's apparent that the oil furnace is used the majority of the time to heat your home, but is supplemented by the pellet stove. I've modelled upgrading to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 97% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



PROPANE FURNACE



Savings:



By replacing your current oil furnace with a 97% AFUE condensing propane furnace that incorporates an ECM fan blower - and considering current oil and propane prices - you could potentially save $1760 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 42 GJ, and qualify for up to $1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP). Please be aware that the modelling is based on using your furnace to supply 100% of your heating needs, which as was mentioned to me during the evaluation, that is not how your home is primarily heated. See below for more information about supplementary heating.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. Please note that to qualify for the EEIP program, a 96% efficient furnace with the components describhed above is all that is required to receive a rebate, but by upgrading to a 97% efficient furnace you would qualify for both the GNWT rebate, and the Canada Greener Homes rebate (see below).



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT:



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $1,600 is available for replacing an oil- or gas-fired furnace with a 97% AFUE ENERGY STAR certified propane-fired furnace. To determine the eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that the unit is included on the Searchable product list of eligible furnaces or boilers for the Canada Greener Homes Grant.



SUPPLEMENTARY HEATING SYSTEM



PELLETS



You provided information during the evaluation that said you regularly use your wood stove to provide heat for your home. I modelled your home to be using your pellet stove to provide 50% of your heating requirements, which showed significant savings of approximately $1760 per year in heating costs, depending on the price of heating oil. 



Another thing you can do to more evenly distribute the heat from your pellet stove (which is intended as a space heater) is to use the furnace fan to circulate the heat. In an older furnace like yours, this will add to your electricity bill in a small way, but if you were to upgrade the fan to an ECM fan, or upgrade to a new furnace including an ECM fan, the heat distribution cost would be minimal.

</HeatingSystem>
                <WaterConservation>

Your house has two older 13 liter toilet that could be replaced with a lower water consumption model. When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 800 - 100 grams per flush!



Savings: By replacing a toilet with a new 4.8 liter or less (or dual-flush) model, you could potentially save ~$50 per person, per year in water and energy savings. 



There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

ADDITIONAL COMMENTS



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020.



Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines.



ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION



For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit.



Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include:



Â In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report

Â If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant.

Â Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit

Â All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional

Â It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that:

     o installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional*

     o an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards

     o the heat pump has been sized to your entire home

*When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory.

Â All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada.



ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES



You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure.



Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources.Programs in your region that you may be eligible for include the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program, administered by the Arctic Energy Alliance. The rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amounts and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test = Pass

The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (kitchen range hood, bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 0.8 Pascals. As this number is lower than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment should not cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your furnace, is an example of this type of appliance.



During my assessment of your home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed and operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.





-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house:

       

        - two bathroom fans

        - a range hood

        - a clothes dryer

      

You may want to consider installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs Â Yellowknife



The fuel values below may be different than current prices, as they have been averaged over the past 5 years to account for fuel price fluctuations over time. These prices have been used to calculate energy costs and energy savings in Yellowknife, for your report:



Electricity - $0.32 per kWh

Oil - $1.24 per liter 

Propane - $0.75 per liter delivered

Wood - $410 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.79 per bag, $351.29 per tonne bulk delivered





-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Scott Dowler, Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: scott.dowler@aea.nt.ca



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!



